WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.940
How does merge sort work?

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.680
Watch these eight numbers. We

3
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.300
will split them, merge them

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.060
in order, and derive the

5
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.060
running time. Do try to

6
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:10.540
keep up.

7
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.560
First, split the array into

8
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.540
two halves, with four elements

9
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.360
in each.

10
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.320
Split each half again. Now

11
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.620
we have four groups of

12
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:20.980
two.

13
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.400
Keep splitting until every group

14
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.700
has one element. A single

15
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.060
element is already sorted. That

16
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.920
is our base case.

17
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.700
Now work back upward. To

18
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.980
merge two sorted groups, compare

19
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.500
their front elements and move

20
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:38.860
the smaller one into the

21
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.060
next output position.

22
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.420
Eight versus three: three moves

23
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.700
first, then eight. Our first

24
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.720
sorted pair is three, eight.

25
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.620
The other pairs merge the

26
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.100
same way: one, five; two,

27
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.740
seven; and four, six.

28
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.920
Merge the first two sorted

29
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.680
pairs. One moves first, then

30
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.140
three, then five. The right

31
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.960
group is empty, so copy

32
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.100
the remaining eight.

33
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.100
Merge the other two pairs.

34
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.660
Two, four, six, then the

35
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.900
remaining seven. Both halves are

36
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:12.560
now sorted.

37
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.200
For the final merge, compare

38
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.660
only the two front elements.

39
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.440
You do not search the

40
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.080
entire groups, obviously.

41
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.860
One beats two. Move one

42
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.500
into the output, and advance

43
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:25.380
the left pointer.

44
00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.380
Three versus two. Move two,

45
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.480
and advance the right pointer.

46
00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.540
Three beats four. Move three

47
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.220
from the left.

48
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.660
Five versus four. Move four

49
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:38.220
from the right.

50
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.120
Five beats six. Move five

51
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:41.860
from the left.

52
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.160
Eight versus six. Move six

53
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:45.900
from the right.

54
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.160
Eight versus seven. Move seven.

55
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.340
The right group is now

56
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:50.740
empty.

57
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.820
Copy the remaining eight. There.

58
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.120
All eight numbers are sorted.

59
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.340
Why is merging linear? Each

60
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.140
element enters the output once.

61
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.420
Across one merge level, all

62
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.180
groups together contain eight elements,

63
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.080
so that level makes eight

64
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.380
output placements. In general, that

65
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.060
is theta of n work.

66
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.480
For eight elements, we merge

67
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.620
pairs, then groups of four,

68
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.160
then all eight: three merge

69
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.320
levels. For n elements, repeated

70
00:02:19.746 --> 00:02:21.900
halving gives about log base

71
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.060
two of n levels.

72
00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.880
Multiply linear work per level

73
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.480
by logarithmic levels: theta of

74
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.020
n log n time. Equivalently,

75
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.660
two half-sized recursive sorts plus

76
00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.240
a linear merge give this

77
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:35.780
recurrence.

78
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.720
Things to take note of

79
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.540
for merge sort. First, the

80
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.280
standard version takes theta of

81
00:02:41.008 --> 00:02:42.300
n log n time in

82
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.700
the best, average, and worst

83
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:44.120
cases.

84
00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.000
Use half-open ranges: start is

85
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.660
included; end is excluded. Stop

86
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.120
when end minus start is

87
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.800
at most one, including an

88
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.120
empty range. Odd lengths split

89
00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.500
into unequal halves.

90
00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.360
Temporary arrays require linear auxiliary

91
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.240
space. Recursion adds a logarithmic

92
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.760
stack. Total extra space stays

93
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.060
linear, so this version is

94
00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:08.940
not in-place.

95
00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.820
On equal keys, take the

96
00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.864
left element first. Here, two

97
00:03:12.864 --> 00:03:14.726
A came before two B,

98
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.580
so the merged output keeps

99
00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.460
A before B. Stability depends

100
00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:19.520
on this tie rule.

101
00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.180
The slides also list order

102
00:03:22.432 --> 00:03:23.800
n log n swaps. In

103
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.160
this implementation, these are element

104
00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.180
copies and writes, rather than

105
00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:31.400
pairwise swaps. Hmph. Remember the

106
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:31.800
details.
